// Deep-dive layer: Ezra, Nehemiah, Esther, Ecclesiastes (ch 5-6). Format as in dd_a.js.
export default {
  14: [
    [1, `Cyrus’s decree, and the Cylinder`,
      `Ezra opens with the Persian king Cyrus announcing that “the LORD, the God of heaven, has given me all the kingdoms of the earth, and he has charged me to build him a house at Jerusalem” (1:2-4), and returns the temple vessels taken by Nebuchadnezzar (1:7-11). The Cyrus Cylinder (539 BCE, in the British Museum) records a general policy of returning gods and peoples to their homelands and restoring their sanctuaries, though it does not mention Jerusalem or Judeans. Isaiah calls Cyrus “his anointed” (Isaiah 45:1), a word used elsewhere only of Israelite kings and priests. The first words of Ezra repeat the last words of 2 Chronicles 36:22-23, linking the two books. Tradition has the first group led by Sheshbazzar, “prince of Judah.”`,
      [[`Isaiah 44:28-45:1`, `Cyrus as shepherd and anointed.`], [`2 Chronicles 36:22-23`, `The same decree, ending Chronicles.`]],
      [`Isaiah calls a foreign emperor God’s “anointed.” What does it do to your idea of who God can use?`, `The Cylinder shows a policy, not a unique favour to Judah. Does that make the biblical claim less or more interesting?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the decree?`, `As a Persian policy that the Bible reads as God’s work.`, `As a theological retelling, which may have improved the decree’s religious language.`]],
    [2, `A list of returnees, and a question of who counts`,
      `Ezra 2 lists nearly 50,000 who return with Zerubbabel and Jeshua, by family and town. Parallel in Nehemiah 7, with small differences in numbers, which scribes have long debated. Among them are 200 singers (Ezra 2:65) and the priestly families who could not find their genealogy and were “excluded from the priesthood as unclean” until a priest could consult the Urim and Thummim (2:61-63). The list gives the community an identity: the names of families and of towns, from Bethlehem to Anathoth. A freed people could be recognised by lineage, which also meant that some were excluded. The question of who belonged would shape the arguments of Ezra and Nehemiah.`,
      [[`Nehemiah 7:5-73`, `The parallel list.`], [`Matthew 1:12`, `Zerubbabel in Jesus’ genealogy.`]],
      [`The list is a mix of belonging and exclusion. Who is left out, and who decides?`, `Why would a community in recovery spend time listing family names?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the list mostly do?`, `Establishes continuity with pre-exilic Israel.`, `Draws boundaries for an identity under pressure.`]],
    [5, `Ezra against the neighbours, and a letter that won`,
      `When the returnees build the temple, local officials write to the Persian court, and Darius orders a search in the archives. A scroll is found in Ecbatana confirming Cyrus’s decree (6:1-5). The text switches from Hebrew to Aramaic for chapters 4:8-6:18 and 7:12-26, in the style of official correspondence. Darius not only allows the work but orders that costs come from the royal treasury and that the temple’s animal sacrifices be provided (6:8-10). The temple is dedicated in 516 BCE, the sixth year of Darius (6:15). The sequence is a story of how bureaucratic documents can be a vehicle of providence. Haggai and Zechariah prophesied during the building (5:1).`,
      [[`Haggai 1:1-11`, `Haggai urges the people to rebuild the house first.`], [`Zechariah 4:6-10`, `“Not by might, nor by power, but by my Spirit.”`]],
      [`The temple’s completion depends on an archived scroll. What does that say about how institutions defend a community?`, `Why would the writer include whole letters, in Aramaic?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main role of the letters?`, `They show the legitimacy of the rebuilding, backed by the empire.`, `They show that God works through administrative structures.`]],
    [7, `Ezra the scribe arrives`,
      `Ezra, “a scribe skilled in the Law of Moses” and a priest descended from Aaron, arrives from Babylon with Artaxerxes’ letter authorising him to teach and enforce “the law of your God” (7:1-26). The text says, “Ezra had set his heart to study the Law of the LORD, and to do it and to teach his statutes and rules in Israel” (7:10), a three-part verse often used as a model for the teacher’s life: study, practise, teach. The date is the seventh year of Artaxerxes, usually 458 BCE (Artaxerxes I), though some scholars place Ezra’s mission later, in 398 BCE (Artaxerxes II). The first-person narration begins at 7:27, as Ezra says, “The hand of the LORD my God was on me.”`,
      [[`Deuteronomy 6:6-9`, `The law on the heart, to be taught.`], [`Nehemiah 8:1-8`, `Ezra reads the law to the assembly.`]],
      [`Study, do, teach: in what order do you tend to approach what you know?`, `Why would an emperor authorise a community’s own law? What did Persia gain?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a personal version of Ezra 7:10, naming one thing you will study, practise and teach this year.`]],
    [8, `Ezra’s fast and a convoy with no escort`,
      `Ezra gathers the exiles at the river Ahava, and finding no Levites, sends for them. He also notes, “I was ashamed to ask the king for a band of soldiers and horsemen to protect us… because we had said to the king, ‘The hand of our God is for good on all who seek him’” (8:22). They fast and pray “for a safe journey” and carry silver, gold and vessels worth a fortune, which he entrusts to twelve priests (8:24-30). The journey takes four months and they arrive safely (8:31-32). The honesty about his own embarrassment is striking: Ezra had made a claim about God that now needed to be tested. The preparation is also careful: weigh the treasure, count it, record it.`,
      [[`Psalm 121:1-8`, `A pilgrim psalm of protection.`], [`Nehemiah 2:7-9`, `By contrast, Nehemiah accepts an escort from the king.`]],
      [`Ezra declines an escort to avoid contradicting his claim. How do you balance reliance on God and sensible precaution?`, `Nehemiah accepts an escort in similar circumstances. Is one right and the other wrong?`],
      [`choose`, `What do you make of the contrast?`, `Each man acts according to his own conviction and context.`, `Ezra’s approach is more admirable because it is more risky.`]],
    [9, `Ezra tears his hair and confesses for others`,
      `Ezra is told that the people, priests and Levites have married foreign women, “the holy race has mixed itself with the peoples of the lands” (9:2). He tears his garment and pulls out hair, sits appalled until the evening sacrifice, then prays on his knees: “O my God, I am ashamed and blush to lift my face to you” (9:6). The prayer says “we,” though Ezra himself is not guilty. Chapter 10 describes a mass divorce of foreign wives. Ruth and Isaiah 56:3-8 show that other voices held a more open view, and Nehemiah 13 repeats the issue. The text records a hard moment for a community afraid of disappearing. Readers still disagree on whether this is faithful or tragic.`,
      [[`Ruth 4:13-17`, `A Moabite woman in David’s line.`], [`Malachi 2:10-16`, `A prophet against both faithlessness and divorce.`]],
      [`Ezra prays “we” for sins he did not commit. What does it mean to confess on behalf of a community?`, `The same canon has Ruth and Ezra 9-10. How should a reader hold both?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read Ezra 9-10?`, `As a painful measure to preserve a fragile identity.`, `As a warning about how fear can harden religion against the vulnerable.`]]
  ],
  15: [
    [1, `Nehemiah’s prayer and a cupbearer’s courage`,
      `In Susa, in the month of Chislev (Dec 445 BCE), Nehemiah hears that the walls of Jerusalem are broken and weeps, fasts and prays (1:4-11). His prayer confesses sin, “I and my father’s house have sinned,” and reminds God of his promise to gather the scattered. Four months later, in Nisan, he serves wine to the king, and his sad face betrays him: “Why is your face sad, seeing you are not sick?” Nehemiah says, “Then I was very much afraid” (2:2). He prays “to the God of heaven” and then answers (2:4). He asks for letters of safe conduct and timber (2:7-8). The prayer in the middle of a conversation shows faith moving between the sanctuary and the court.`,
      [[`Daniel 9:4-19`, `Another exilic confession in the first person plural.`], [`Proverbs 21:1`, `“The king’s heart is a stream of water in the hand of the LORD.”`]],
      [`Nehemiah prays for four months and then acts in a moment of fear. How do prayer and courage work together here?`, `A sad face is a risk in a royal court. When has an honest expression cost you something?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a one-line “arrow prayer” like Nehemiah’s in 2:4, for a moment when you need to answer a powerful person.`]],
    [3, `A wall built by dozens of hands`,
      `Chapter 3 lists the builders of the wall, gate by gate: priests, goldsmiths, perfumers, a ruler of half a district “and his daughters” (3:12), the men of Tekoa, though “their nobles would not stoop to serve their Lord” (3:5). Many repaired the section opposite their own house (3:10, 23, 28-30). The list gives names to a project often credited to one man. Archaeology has found a stretch of what may be the “Broad Wall” (from Hezekiah’s time), and the line of the Persian-period wall on the eastern ridge is narrower, built hastily. The chapter models shared work: each takes a portion, and the details of who worked where are preserved because each contribution mattered.`,
      [[`1 Corinthians 12:12-27`, `Paul’s image of the body with many parts.`], [`Nehemiah 4:6`, `“The people had a mind to work.”`]],
      [`Each builder repairs the section by their own house. What is the principle behind that arrangement?`, `The nobles of Tekoa refused to work. What does the record of their refusal do?`],
      [`rate`, `How willing are you to take on a section of work no one will notice?`, `Not willing`, `Very willing`]],
    [6, `Four invitations to stop`,
      `Sanballat and Geshem send four messages to Nehemiah inviting him to meet in the plain of Ono (6:1-4), and Nehemiah replies each time, “I am doing a great work and I cannot come down. Why should the work stop?” The fifth is an open letter accusing him of planning rebellion and a false prophet’s warning that he should hide in the temple, which Nehemiah rejects: “Should such a man as I run away? And what man such as I could go into the temple and live?” (6:11). The wall is finished in fifty-two days (6:15), and “all the nations around us were afraid and fell greatly in their own esteem.” The chapter shows how distraction, slander and fear are used to halt good work.`,
      [[`Luke 9:51`, `Jesus “set his face” toward Jerusalem.`], [`Galatians 6:9`, `“Let us not grow weary of doing good.”`]],
      [`“I am doing a great work and I cannot come down.” What invitation have you declined to protect your work?`, `A friendly meeting can be a trap. How do you tell?`],
      [`choose`, `What stands out about Nehemiah’s response?`, `His singleness of purpose.`, `His ability to identify manipulation and pray through it.`]],
    [7, `A guard on the gates and a list found`,
      `With the wall finished, Nehemiah appoints his brother Hanani and Hananiah the commander of the citadel, “for he was a more faithful and God-fearing man than many” (7:2), and gives orders that the gates be opened late in the morning and shut early, with guards from the residents. The city is large but few are in it and “no houses had been rebuilt” (7:4). God puts it in his heart to register the people, and he finds the genealogy of the first returnees (7:5-73), parallel to Ezra 2. The practical challenge, a walled city with no population, leads to the casting of lots to bring one in ten to live inside (11:1). A city needs people, not just stones.`,
      [[`Nehemiah 11:1-2`, `One in ten settles in Jerusalem by lot.`], [`Ezra 2:1-70`, `The earlier version of the same list.`]],
      [`A wall without residents is a monument, not a city. What does that say about completed projects?`, `Nehemiah chooses leaders by faithfulness and fear of God. What do you look for?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the point of the chapter?`, `Building must be followed by repopulation.`, `The list shows continuity with the earlier return.`]],
    [9, `A national confession`,
      `After the Feast of Booths, the people assemble in sackcloth and read the law for a quarter of the day, confess for another quarter, and a long prayer of the Levites retells the story from creation through Abraham, the exodus, Sinai, wilderness, conquest, judges and kings (9:5-37). The prayer is a historical psalm like Psalm 105-106. Its refrain is “but they… yet you”: they rebelled, yet you were merciful (9:16-31). It closes with a line of present honesty: “Behold, we are slaves today; in the land that you gave to our fathers… we are slaves” (9:36). The Persian Empire’s taxes weigh on the produce. The prayer ends with a binding agreement.`,
      [[`Psalm 106:6-46`, `A parallel confession psalm.`], [`Acts 7:2-53`, `Stephen’s speech, another retelling of Israel’s story.`]],
      [`The prayer says “we are slaves today.” What does it mean to confess honestly about political reality?`, `Why retell the whole story in a confession?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a short “they … yet you” confession of your own community’s story, in four lines.`]],
    [10, `The signed covenant`,
      `The leaders put their seals to a binding agreement (10:1-27) to walk in God’s law, not to marry foreigners, not to trade on the Sabbath, to observe the seventh year, to pay a third of a shekel yearly for the temple, to provide wood for the altar, and to bring firstfruits and tithes (10:28-39). It ends: “We will not neglect the house of our God.” The phrase appears again in 13:11. The covenant is notable because it is specific: regulations, taxes and trade practice. The Sabbath clause is directed at foreign merchants who sell goods on the Sabbath. The idea of putting one’s seal to a promise shows a community choosing obligations in writing.`,
      [[`Nehemiah 13:15-22`, `Nehemiah enforces the Sabbath when merchants return.`], [`Exodus 24:3-8`, `The earlier covenant sealed with an oath and blood.`]],
      [`A promise that names specific practices is harder to avoid than one that names ideals. What would a specific version of your commitments look like?`, `Why does the covenant include money for the temple’s upkeep?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the covenant?`, `A renewal that translates ideals into policy.`, `An attempt to create social unity under strict rules.`]],
    [11, `The city by lot`,
      `Chapter 11 reports that leaders settled in Jerusalem, and the rest of the people cast lots to bring one in ten to live there, while others settled in surrounding towns (11:1-2). “And the people blessed all the men who willingly offered to live in Jerusalem.” The chapter lists those who lived in the city, including Judahites and Benjaminites, priests, Levites, gatekeepers and singers. The language of volunteering and lots suggests that some moved from duty and others by choice. The chapter gives a vision of a holy city: Jerusalem as the sanctuary city, which makes the settlement an act of dedication.`,
      [[`1 Chronicles 9:1-34`, `The parallel list of Jerusalem’s residents.`], [`Nehemiah 7:4`, `Few people in the city.`]],
      [`Some were chosen by lot to live in the capital, and others volunteered. How do you view moves of duty versus those of choice?`, `The people bless the volunteers. Who gets blessed in your community?`],
      [`rate`, `How willing are you to relocate your life for a shared cause?`, `Not willing`, `Very willing`]],
    [12, `The wall is dedicated with two choirs`,
      `Chapter 12 lists priests and Levites from the days of Zerubbabel and then describes the wall’s dedication. Two large choirs are formed and set off in opposite directions along the top of the wall, one led by Ezra and the other by Nehemiah, and meet at the temple (12:31-40). “And they offered great sacrifices that day and rejoiced, for God had made them rejoice with great joy; the women and children also rejoiced. And the joy of Jerusalem was heard far away” (12:43). The structure is a procession in the shape of a circuit of the city. Psalms such as 48:12-13 invite the pilgrim to walk around Zion and count its towers. The wall becomes a liturgical space.`,
      [[`Psalm 48:12-14`, `“Walk about Zion… consider well her ramparts.”`], [`Joshua 6:1-20`, `Another procession around a city’s walls, with a different purpose.`]],
      [`The wall is dedicated by singing as well as stones. What does a dedication add to a finished building?`, `Two choirs set off in opposite directions and meet. What does the image suggest?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Describe a modern ritual to dedicate something your community has built, with a procession, song and a closing meal.`]]
  ],
  16: [
    [1, `A banquet and a queen who says no`,
      `Esther opens with Xerxes (Ahasuerus) holding a six-month display of wealth and then a seven-day feast. On the seventh day, he calls Queen Vashti to appear wearing her royal crown “to show the peoples and the princes her beauty” (1:11). She refuses. His counsellors warn that her refusal will inspire all women to disrespect their husbands, and she is deposed. Many readers have found Vashti’s “no” an act of dignity, although the book does not comment. The name of God is never mentioned in the book; the Greek version adds prayers and references to God. The book shows a court of excess, with empire shaped by whim and law that cannot be repealed (1:19).`,
      [[`Esther 4:14`, `“If you keep silent at such a time as this.”`], [`Daniel 6:8-15`, `Another Persian law that cannot be revoked.`]],
      [`How do you read Vashti’s refusal? Is the narrator on her side?`, `The book never names God. What do you make of a sacred text that is silent on God?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the best reading of Vashti?`, `A model of dignity that sets up the story of Esther’s courage.`, `A foil whose fate shows the dangers women face in a court.`]],
    [8, `A reversal by decree`,
      `Haman is dead, but his edict to destroy the Jews cannot be revoked. Mordecai is given the king’s signet ring, and the Jews are authorised to defend themselves on the thirteenth of Adar (8:1-14). The new decree is sent to 127 provinces “in the script of each province and the language of each people.” When the day comes, many in the empire “declared themselves Jews, for fear of the Jews had fallen on them” (8:17). The reversal is the book’s main motif: the gallows built for Mordecai are used for Haman, and the day of doom becomes the festival of Purim. The violence that follows in chapter 9, with 75,000 enemies killed, troubles many readers and has been read as a reversal fantasy.`,
      [[`Esther 9:20-32`, `The institution of Purim.`], [`Proverbs 26:27`, `“Whoever digs a pit will fall into it.”`]],
      [`The law cannot be revoked, so the solution is a counter-law. What does that say about how power works?`, `How do you read the violence of Esther 9?`],
      [`choose`, `How should a modern reader approach Esther 8-9?`, `As a story of survival, whose violence is defensive.`, `As a text that reveals the dangers of retributive reversal fantasies.`]]
  ],
  17: [
    [4, `Eliphaz’s night vision`,
      `Eliphaz opens the first cycle of the friends’ speeches with a gentle rebuke and a claim to a vision: “A spirit glided past my face; the hair of my flesh stood up” (4:15). It says, “Can mortal man be in the right before God? Can a man be pure before his Maker?” (4:17). He argues that the innocent do not perish (4:7), a premise Job’s story contradicts. The vision is true as theology (humans are not pure before God) but misapplied to Job. Eliphaz’s speech is often admired and misused: parts of it are quoted in the New Testament (1 Corinthians 3:19 quotes Job 5:13). The book’s epilogue says God was angry with Eliphaz and his friends, “for you have not spoken of me what is right, as my servant Job has” (42:7).`,
      [[`Job 42:7-9`, `God’s verdict on the friends.`], [`1 Corinthians 3:19`, `Paul quotes Eliphaz (Job 5:13).`]],
      [`A speech can be true in parts and wrong in application. How do you tell in the case of comforters?`, `Eliphaz claims a vision. How should one weigh religious experience in giving advice?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main error of Eliphaz?`, `He applies a general principle to a specific suffering.`, `He presents his experience as authoritative and leaves no room for Job’s voice.`]],
    [5, `The friend’s advice, and a half-quoted line`,
      `Eliphaz continues with advice: “As for me, I would seek God, and to God would I commit my cause” (5:8), then a list of God’s works that includes a line often quoted alone: “Behold, blessed is the one whom God reproves; therefore despise not the discipline of the Almighty” (5:17). He ends with a promise that Job will be safe from famine, sword and wild beasts, “you shall know that your tent is at peace” (5:24). The speech is full of well-formed wisdom. Yet it assumes suffering is correction. The reader knows from the prologue that this is not the case. Job’s answer in chapters 6-7 is that his friends are like seasonal streams that dry up in summer (6:15-20).`,
      [[`Job 1:6-12`, `The prologue, which shows Job’s suffering has a different cause.`], [`Hebrews 12:5-11`, `The idea of discipline as a sign of sonship, used in a different setting.`]],
      [`Eliphaz’s advice is an orthodox, comforting theology. Why does it fail Job?`, `When has someone offered you a theological explanation for a pain that did not fit?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Rewrite Eliphaz’s advice as it might be given by a well-meaning friend today, and then write Job’s answer in a sentence.`]],
    [6, `Job: “My brothers are treacherous as a torrent-bed”`,
      `Job answers that his complaint is heavy “like the sand of the seas” and that his friends fail him: “He who withholds kindness from a friend forsakes the fear of the Almighty” (6:14). He compares them to wadis that are full in winter and vanish in the heat, so caravans turn aside and die (6:15-20). He asks for plain speech: “Teach me, and I will be silent; make me understand how I have gone astray” (6:24). Then he adds: “How forceful are upright words! But what does reproof from you reprove?” (6:25). The speech shows Job’s main complaint: not his pain alone but his friends’ failure to listen.`,
      [[`Job 2:11-13`, `The friends’ first act: seven days of silence.`], [`Romans 12:15`, `“Weep with those who weep.”`]],
      [`The friends were at their best when they were silent. What does that suggest about comfort?`, `Job asks to be shown where he is wrong. Is that honest or defensive?`],
      [`rate`, `When someone you love is hurting, how well do you listen before advising?`, `Poorly`, `Well`]],
    [7, `“What is man, that you make so much of him?”`,
      `Job’s speech in chapter 7 is a parody of Psalm 8: “What is man, that you make so much of him, and that you set your heart on him, visiting him every morning and testing him every moment?” (7:17-18). In the psalm, humans are crowned with glory; here God’s attention is a burden. Job cries, “Let me alone, for my days are a breath” (7:16). He names his sleepless nights, worms in the flesh, and “my life is a breath” (7:7). The poem uses the language of worship against itself. Its presence in Scripture is a permission: honest complaint to God is a form of faith. Psalm 88 is another example, ending in darkness.`,
      [[`Psalm 8:3-8`, `The psalm Job echoes in reverse.`], [`Psalm 88:1-18`, `A lament that ends without hope.`]],
      [`Job turns a psalm of praise into a complaint. What does it mean that Scripture preserves this?`, `When have you wanted God to look away?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read Job’s parody?`, `As an honest, faithful protest.`, `As a bitter outburst that the book later corrects.`]],
    [9, `Who can argue with God?`,
      `Job’s answer to Bildad opens with the question that drives the book: “How can a man be in the right before God?” (9:2). He describes a God who moves mountains, commands the sun, and made the Bear, Orion and the Pleiades (9:5-10). If he were to summon God to court, “who could answer him one in a thousand?” (9:3). He longs for “a mediator between us, who might lay his hand on us both” (9:33), a wish later echoed in Job 16:19-21 and 19:25. The image of the lawsuit shapes the book: Job as plaintiff, God as defendant, and no judge. Christians have read the “mediator” longing in light of Christ (1 Timothy 2:5).`,
      [[`Job 19:25-27`, `“I know that my Redeemer lives.”`], [`1 Timothy 2:5`, `“One mediator between God and men.”`]],
      [`Job wants an umpire between himself and God. What does that say about his sense of the divine?`, `Can you ask a question of God when the questioner cannot win? Why ask?`],
      [`predict`, `Before reading chapters 38-41, predict whether God will answer Job’s legal challenge and how.`]],
    [10, `Job asks why he was born`,
      `Job’s speech in chapter 10 is a long address to God: “Does it seem good to you to oppress, to despise the work of your hands?” (10:3), followed by an image of creation as intimacy turned to threat: “Your hands fashioned and made me… Remember that you have made me like clay; and will you return me to the dust?” (10:8-9). He describes being formed in the womb like cheese curdled from milk (10:10), an image of the process of birth, and asks why he was brought forth. The prayer ends in a request for a little brightness before he goes to “the land of gloom and deep darkness” (10:21). The speech holds together tenderness and accusation in one complaint.`,
      [[`Psalm 139:13-16`, `The psalm of intimate formation, used here ironically.`], [`Jeremiah 20:14-18`, `Jeremiah’s curse on his birthday, another voice that asks why.`]],
      [`Job reads creation as if God’s care became a trap. How does that compare with Psalm 139?`, `What do you do with prayers that sound closer to accusation than praise?`],
      [`choose`, `Is Job’s speech a breach of faith?`, `No: he addresses God directly, even in complaint.`, `Yes, partly, since he claims knowledge of God’s motives that he does not have.`]],
    [12, `“Ask the animals, and they will teach you”`,
      `Job answers Zophar with sarcasm: “No doubt you are the people, and wisdom will die with you” (12:2). He then turns the friends’ theology back on them: “Ask the beasts, and they will teach you; the birds of the heavens, and they will tell you… Who among all these does not know that the hand of the LORD has done this?” (12:7-9). He says God overturns the mighty: “He leads counsellors away stripped, and judges he makes fools” (12:17). The same God who creates also destroys, and the world gives evidence of both. Job’s point is that wisdom is not the friends’ monopoly: he knows what they know and sees the facts they ignore.`,
      [[`Proverbs 6:6-8`, `“Go to the ant, O sluggard.”`], [`Isaiah 1:3`, `“The ox knows its owner.”`]],
      [`Job says the animals can teach about God. What do you learn from the non-human world?`, `He accuses his friends of ignoring the evidence of unjust power. How does that shape the book’s argument?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write four lines in which an animal, plant or landscape teaches something about God that your tradition has not.`]],
    [13, `“Though he slay me”`,
      `Job demands an audience: “Behold, he will slay me; I have no hope; yet I will argue my ways to his face” (13:15). The Hebrew is ambiguous: the written text has *lo* (“not”), while the traditional reading has *lo* (“in him”), so it can mean “I have no hope” or “I will hope in him,” and the second shaped the King James: “Though he slay me, yet will I trust in him.” Either reading fits the book: Job’s stubborn trust in God’s justice drives him to challenge God. He tells the friends they are “worthless physicians” and “whitewashers of lies” (13:4) who speak falsely for God. “Will you speak unrighteously for God?” (13:7). The point is stern: a defence of God that lies is not a defence.`,
      [[`Job 42:7`, `God agrees that the friends did not speak rightly.`], [`Habakkuk 3:17-19`, `“Yet I will rejoice in the LORD.”`]],
      [`“Will you speak unrighteously for God?” Where do you see this happen today?`, `Job both hopes and argues. How can a person do both?`],
      [`choose`, `Which reading of 13:15 do you prefer?`, `“Though he slay me, I will hope in him.”`, `“Behold, he will slay me; I have no hope.”`]],
    [14, `A tree that sprouts again, a man who does not`,
      `Job laments that “man who is born of a woman is few of days and full of trouble” (14:1) and compares human death with a tree: “there is hope for a tree, if it be cut down, that it will sprout again… But a man dies and is laid low” (14:7-10). He then wishes that God would hide him in Sheol until his wrath passed, and “set me a fixed time, and remember me!” (14:13). “If a man dies, shall he live again? All the days of my service I would wait, till my renewal should come” (14:14). The text raises the possibility of resurrection and then withdraws it. The question is not resolved, but it is the first time in the book that hope for life after death appears as a wish.`,
      [[`Daniel 12:2`, `The first clear statement of resurrection in the Hebrew Bible.`], [`1 Corinthians 15:20-26`, `Paul’s answer to the question of whether the dead will live.`]],
      [`Job asks, “If a man dies, shall he live again?” and does not receive an answer here. What do you do with a question that the book leaves open?`, `A tree can come back from a stump. What does the image offer?`],
      [`choose`, `What is Job doing in 14:13-15?`, `Imagining resurrection as a possibility.`, `Expressing a wish that God’s anger could be suspended.`]],
    [15, `Eliphaz hardens`,
      `In the second cycle, the friends sharpen. Eliphaz says, “Are you the first man who was born?” (15:7) and charges Job with undermining piety: “Your own mouth condemns you” (15:6). He repeats the theory that the wicked suffer: “The wicked man writhes in pain all his days” (15:20). The speech reveals what the friends are defending: a moral order in which people get what they deserve. Challenging that order would unsettle their world. The friends’ tone changes from comfort to accusation as Job refuses to concede. The pattern of escalating attack on the person who questions a theology recurs in religious communities.`,
      [[`John 9:1-3`, `Jesus’ disciples ask who sinned, and Jesus rejects the framework.`], [`Luke 13:1-5`, `Jesus on the Galileans killed by Pilate.`]],
      [`The friends defend a moral order. What do they lose if Job is innocent?`, `Where have you seen a theory defended by blaming a sufferer?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the friends’ deepest fear?`, `That the world is not just.`, `That they themselves could suffer in the same way.`]],
    [16, `“My witness is in heaven”`,
      `Job calls his friends “miserable comforters” (16:2) and describes God as a hostile warrior who has “torn me in his wrath” (16:9). Then he makes a surprising appeal: “Earth, cover not my blood… Even now, behold, my witness is in heaven, and he who testifies for me is on high” (16:18-19). “My intercessor is my friend, as my eye pours out tears to God” (16:20). The speech stretches a paradox: God is Job’s enemy and Job’s only hope of vindication, so he appeals from God to God. The same move appears in 19:25-27. The image of blood crying from the earth recalls Abel (Genesis 4:10).`,
      [[`Genesis 4:10`, `Abel’s blood cries from the ground.`], [`Romans 8:34`, `Christ “who is at the right hand of God… intercedes for us.”`]],
      [`Job appeals from God to God. How does one do that in prayer?`, `How do you comfort someone without trying to explain their suffering?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write two sentences you would say to a friend in Job’s position, avoiding explanation.`]],
    [18, `Bildad paints the end of the wicked`,
      `Bildad’s second speech is a catalogue of the destruction of the wicked: “the light of the wicked is put out… his own schemes throw him down… a trap seizes him” (18:5-10). “Surely such are the dwellings of the unrighteous” (18:21). Every image of snare, net and darkness is aimed at Job without naming him. The speech assumes that disaster signals guilt. It also shows how a theory can become cruelty: describing a sufferer’s state as a general principle is an accusation. Job’s answer (19:2-5) is “How long will you torment me and break me in pieces with words?” A pattern of speech becomes a form of violence.`,
      [[`Psalm 1:4-6`, `The psalm of the way of the righteous and the way of the wicked.`], [`Proverbs 10:27-30`, `Wisdom sayings of the same type.`]],
      [`Bildad’s teaching is common in Proverbs and the Psalms. Why does it become harmful in Job?`, `When does a general rule become a weapon?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main problem with Bildad’s speech?`, `He generalises too much.`, `He uses theology to avoid the person in front of him.`]],
    [20, `Zophar’s speech and the world that does not work like that`,
      `Zophar insists that “the exulting of the wicked is short, and the joy of the godless but for a moment” (20:5), and that stolen riches will be vomited up (20:15). Job’s reply in chapter 21 is blunt: “Why do the wicked live, reach old age, and grow mighty in power?… Their houses are safe from fear” (21:7-9). He points to observed facts that the friends must ignore. “How often is it that the lamp of the wicked is put out?” (21:17). The exchange stages the book’s central argument between retribution theology and lived experience. Ecclesiastes 8:14 will make a similar observation: “there are righteous people to whom it happens according to the deeds of the wicked.”`,
      [[`Ecclesiastes 8:14`, `The vanity that the righteous and wicked do not always get what they deserve.`], [`Psalm 73:3-12`, `Asaph’s envy at the prosperity of the wicked.`]],
      [`Job argues from evidence. How often do you test a theology against what you see?`, `What does it cost a community to admit that the wicked sometimes prosper?`],
      [`choose`, `Who is more honest here?`, `Job, who looks at the world as it is.`, `Zophar, who holds to a moral order that gives meaning.`]],
    [21, `Job’s tally of the wicked who die in peace`,
      `Job offers a counter-list: the wicked “spend their days in prosperity, and in peace they go down to Sheol” (21:13), their children play, their cattle breed, and they say to God, “Depart from us!” (21:14). He also notes that the fortune of the righteous is not guaranteed, and that “one dies in his full vigour… another dies in bitterness of soul, never having tasted prosperity. They lie down alike in the dust” (21:23-26). He charges the friends with ignoring the experience of travellers (21:29). The speech ends: “How then will you comfort me with empty nothings? There is nothing left of your answers but falsehood” (21:34). Job insists on honesty about what he sees.`,
      [[`Psalm 73:12-14`, `The same observation in a psalm.`], [`Jeremiah 12:1-2`, `“Why does the way of the wicked prosper?”`]],
      [`Job insists that the evidence disproves the friends’ theology. Does evidence always settle a religious question?`, `What does it mean to “comfort with empty nothings”?`],
      [`choose`, `What does Job’s argument establish?`, `That the theory of strict retribution is false.`, `That the theory cannot explain every case, and humility is needed.`]],
    [22, `Eliphaz invents Job’s sins`,
      `Eliphaz, running out of arguments, creates charges: “Is not your evil abundant? There is no end to your iniquities. For you have exacted pledges of your brothers for nothing… you have given no water to the weary… you have sent widows away empty” (22:5-9). None of this appears in the prologue, where Job is “blameless and upright” (1:1). Chapter 31 is Job’s reply, a detailed oath of innocence that answers each charge in turn. Eliphaz also offers a hopeful close: “Agree with God, and be at peace; thereby good will come to you” (22:21). The way from false accusation to false comfort illustrates how a theory can corrupt a good man’s perception.`,
      [[`Job 31:16-23`, `Job’s denial of the very charges Eliphaz makes.`], [`Exodus 20:16`, `“You shall not bear false witness.”`]],
      [`Eliphaz invents sins to fit a theory. How does that happen in real life?`, `Job’s answer in chapter 31 is a detailed oath. Why does it matter that he lists specific acts?`],
      [`rate`, `How likely are you to assume a person’s suffering is their fault?`, `Unlikely`, `Likely`]],
    [23, `“If only I knew where to find him”`,
      `Job turns from the friends: “Oh, that I knew where I might find him, that I might come even to his seat!” (23:3). He imagines a hearing: “I would lay my case before him and fill my mouth with arguments” (23:4), and he believes God would listen. But “if I go forward, he is not there, and backward, I do not perceive him” (23:8). Yet he affirms: “He knows the way that I take; when he has tried me, I shall come out as gold” (23:10). The speech holds the absence of God and confidence in God’s knowledge of him together. The line “I shall come out as gold” became a common image of suffering as refining, used in 1 Peter 1:7.`,
      [[`Psalm 139:7-10`, `“Where shall I flee from your presence?” The opposite experience.`], [`1 Peter 1:6-7`, `Faith tested like gold.`]],
      [`Job says God is absent and also that God knows him. How do you hold both?`, `Is “I shall come out as gold” a statement of confidence or of defiance?`],
      [`choose`, `What does Job want most?`, `A hearing before God.`, `An answer to why he suffers.`]],
    [25, `The question Job’s friends never ask`,
      `Bildad’s last speech is only six verses (25:1-6): “How can man be in the right before God? How can he who is born of woman be pure? Behold, even the moon is not bright, and the stars are not pure in his eyes; how much less man, who is a maggot, and the son of man, who is a worm!” Job responds in chapters 26-27, mocking the friends’ help and swearing that he will not abandon his integrity: “As long as my breath is in me… my lips will not speak falsehood… till I die I will not put away my integrity from me” (27:3-5). The friends’ speeches get shorter as Job’s resolve grows. The shrinking of Bildad’s speech suggests a position running out of room.`,
      [[`Psalm 8:3-5`, `The same human smallness, with a different conclusion.`], [`Job 27:1-6`, `Job’s oath of integrity.`]],
      [`Bildad’s speech shrinks to a fragment. What does that tell you about the argument?`, `Humans are small, in Bildad’s view, and in Psalm 8. What makes the difference between the two?`],
      [`choose`, `What is Job’s integrity?`, `His refusal to lie about himself to satisfy a theory.`, `His claim to be sinless, which the book tests.`]],
    [29, `Job remembers`,
      `After the poem on wisdom in chapter 28 (“Where shall wisdom be found?”), Job recalls his former life: “Oh, that I were as in the months of old, as in the days when God watched over me” (29:2). He remembers sitting at the city gate, honoured by elders, and acting as a defender: “I was eyes to the blind and feet to the lame. I was a father to the needy” (29:15-16). His status came from justice, not wealth. Then in chapter 30 he says that the young men he would not have set with the dogs of his flock now mock him. The contrast of before and after makes the picture of loss concrete. Chapter 31 will be his oath of innocence, and the book turns toward God’s answer.`,
      [[`Job 28:12-28`, `The poem on wisdom, ending with “the fear of the Lord, that is wisdom.”`], [`Psalm 72:12-14`, `The ideal king as a defender of the poor, a pattern Job fits.`]],
      [`Job’s nostalgia is for a life of service, not only comfort. What would you most miss if you lost everything?`, `Poem 28 declares that wisdom cannot be found by humans. How does it prepare for the speeches that follow?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a short passage in the style of Job 29 about a role you once held and what justice you hoped to do in it.`]],
    [30, `The low point`,
      `Chapter 30 is Job’s lowest: those who were once beneath him mock him, “they spit at the sight of me” (30:10). He cries to God and says that God does not answer: “I stand, and you only look at me. You have turned cruel to me” (30:20-21). He compares himself to a brother of jackals, a companion of ostriches (30:29). The only light in the chapter is a question: “Did not I weep for him whose day was hard? Was not my soul grieved for the needy?” (30:25). He ends in darkness: “My lyre is turned to mourning” (30:31). It is a poem about shame, perhaps the hardest of Job’s experiences: social collapse as well as physical pain.`,
      [[`Psalm 22:1-8`, `A psalm of abandonment and mockery.`], [`Mark 15:29-32`, `Jesus is mocked on the cross, as Job is mocked.`]],
      [`Job names shame as a core part of his suffering. How does shame deepen pain?`, `He remembers his own compassion. Why mention it here?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the chapter show?`, `The limits of endurance and the honesty of lament.`, `A preparation for the final answer from the whirlwind.`]],
    [31, `Job’s oath of innocence`,
      `Chapter 31 is a long “if I have…” oath, listing sins Job denies: lust (31:1), falsehood, adultery (31:9), mistreating a servant (31:13), refusing food to the poor (31:16-20), trusting in gold (31:24), idolatry of sun and moon (31:26-27), revenge on enemies (31:29), withholding hospitality from strangers (31:32), and abusing his own land (31:38-40). The standard is high, closer to the Sermon on the Mount than to ritual law. Many interpreters regard it as one of the great ethical portraits in the Bible. Job ends with a signature: “Here is my signature! Let the Almighty answer me!” (31:35). Then “The words of Job are ended.”`,
      [[`Matthew 5:27-30`, `Jesus on lust and the heart.`], [`Job 31:13-15`, `A servant has the same Maker: an ethic of equality.`]],
      [`Job’s ethic includes how he treats servants, strangers and enemies. How does it compare with your own?`, `He signs the document and asks for an answer. What does that boldness say about his faith?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write your own oath of five “if I have…” clauses on the ethic you most want to be tested on.`]],
    [32, `Elihu bursts in`,
      `A young man, Elihu, who has waited because of his age, speaks in chapters 32-37. He is angry with Job for justifying himself rather than God and with the friends for failing to answer him (32:2-3). He says, “It is not the old who are wise, nor the aged who understand what is right” (32:9) and claims, “I am full of words; the spirit within me constrains me” (32:18). He is not mentioned in the prologue or epilogue, and some scholars regard the Elihu speeches as a later insertion, while others see them as a deliberate bridge. His voice introduces a theology of suffering as warning and instruction, and he anticipates God’s speech with images of storms and rain.`,
      [[`Job 38:1`, `God speaks from the whirlwind after Elihu.`], [`Ecclesiastes 12:11-12`, `Words of the wise as goads.`]],
      [`Elihu is unmentioned by God in the epilogue. What do you make of that silence?`, `Is his claim that youth can see what age cannot a gift or arrogance?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read Elihu?`, `As a later addition with a different theology.`, `As a deliberate part of the book’s design, preparing for God’s answer.`]],
    [33, `Elihu: God speaks in dreams and in pain`,
      `Elihu argues that God does speak, but humans do not perceive: “For God speaks in one way, and in two, though man does not perceive it” (33:14). He lists dreams that frighten a person from pride, and pain on a sickbed, where an angel-mediator may intervene: “then he is gracious to him, and says, ‘Deliver him from going down into the pit; I have found a ransom’” (33:24). The person then prays, and “he sees his face with a shout of joy.” Elihu’s view is more hopeful than the friends’: suffering as a messenger, not only punishment. Yet it also treats pain as a lesson. The idea of a “ransom” links the chapter to later reflections on atonement.`,
      [[`Mark 10:45`, `The Son of Man gives his life as “a ransom for many.”`], [`Hebrews 12:5-11`, `Discipline as a mark of sonship.`]],
      [`Elihu allows that suffering can be a message from God. When is that helpful and when is it harmful?`, `The “mediator” and “ransom” appear here. How does the book’s theology of mediation develop?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the best reading of Elihu’s view?`, `A gentler, pedagogical theology of suffering.`, `A softened version of the friends’ argument.`]],
    [34, `Elihu says God cannot do wrong`,
      `Elihu insists that “far be it from God that he should do wickedness… For according to the work of a man he repays him” (34:10-11). He accuses Job of “drinking up scoffing like water” (34:7) and adding rebellion to sin (34:37). He also affirms God’s sovereignty: “If he should set his heart to it and gather to himself his spirit and his breath, all flesh would perish together” (34:14-15). The speech defends God’s justice in the same terms the friends used, and its weakness is that it explains little about Job’s case. It sets out a view of God as impartial: he “shows no partiality to princes” (34:19). The poem anticipates Paul’s language in Romans 2:11.`,
      [[`Romans 2:11`, `“For God shows no partiality.”`], [`Genesis 18:25`, `“Shall not the Judge of all the earth do what is just?”`]],
      [`Elihu defends God’s justice but does not address Job’s experience. What does that leave out?`, `How do you affirm God’s justice without denying real suffering?`],
      [`choose`, `What do you make of Elihu’s defence?`, `It states an important truth that can be used unfeelingly.`, `It misses the point, as the book’s ending suggests.`]],
    [35, `Elihu on prayer that goes unanswered`,
      `Elihu asks why people cry out under oppression and “none says, ‘Where is God my Maker, who gives songs in the night?’” (35:10). He argues that God does not answer “because of the pride of evil men” (35:12). He also tells Job that his sin or righteousness affects only himself and other humans, not God (35:6-8). The speech touches an important question: why do sufferers cry out and receive no answer? The “songs in the night” line has been used widely in Christian devotion. Elihu’s answer, that pride blocks prayer, is partial, and Job will receive God’s answer without being told he was proud.`,
      [[`Psalm 42:8`, `“At night his song is with me.”`], [`Acts 16:25`, `Paul and Silas sing hymns in prison at midnight.`]],
      [`“Where is God my Maker, who gives songs in the night?” How would you answer?`, `Elihu says God’s power is not affected by human sin. Is that comforting or cold?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a short “song in the night” for a time of unanswered prayer, four lines in your own words.`]],
    [36, `Elihu points to the storm`,
      `Elihu argues that God “does not despise any, for he is mighty in strength of understanding” (36:5) and “opens their ear to instruction” through affliction (36:10). Then his speech turns toward nature: “Behold, God is great, and we know him not; the number of his years is unsearchable” (36:26). He describes water drawn up as mist and poured out as rain, and thunder as God’s voice (36:27-33). This leads straight into chapter 37, in which he trembles at the thunder and says, “The Almighty—we cannot find him; he is great in power; justice and abundant righteousness he will not violate” (37:23). Elihu’s speeches serve as a transition: the storm he describes comes in the next scene, when God answers.`,
      [[`Job 38:1-3`, `God answers from the whirlwind.`], [`Psalm 29:3-9`, `The “voice of the LORD” in the storm.`]],
      [`Elihu’s speech leads to the storm and God’s voice. How does the placement help you read it?`, `He says God is beyond finding out. What does that mean for a person in pain?`],
      [`predict`, `Before reading chapter 38, predict what God will say to Job and whether he will explain the suffering.`]],
    [37, `“We cannot find him”`,
      `Elihu’s final speech is about thunder, snow, rain, cold and the clouds: “God thunders wondrously with his voice; he does great things that we cannot comprehend” (37:5). The weather declares God’s rule: “He loads the clouds with moisture… whether for correction, or for his land, or for love” (37:12-13). He ends: “Teach us what we shall say to him; we cannot draw up our case because of darkness” (37:19). The last verse: “The Almighty—we cannot find him; he is great in power; justice and abundant righteousness he will not violate. Therefore men fear him” (37:23-24). The chapter is both a conclusion and a preparation for the divine speeches: Elihu names what Job is about to experience.`,
      [[`Psalm 147:15-18`, `God’s word sends snow and ice.`], [`1 Kings 19:11-13`, `Elijah’s encounter with God in wind, earthquake, fire and a still small voice.`]],
      [`Elihu’s weather poems end in humility: “we cannot find him.” How does this differ from the friends’ confidence?`, `What is it like to realise you cannot draw up your case?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the end of the Elihu speeches?`, `A turn from argument toward wonder.`, `A final attempt to defend God by magnifying his greatness.`]],
    [39, `God’s questions about the wild`,
      `God’s second round of questions takes Job into the wild: the mountain goats giving birth, the wild donkey who laughs at the city, the wild ox who will not serve, the ostrich who “forgets” her eggs and yet laughs at the horseman, the war horse who “smells the battle from afar,” the hawk and the eagle (39:1-30). The creatures are untamed and unaccountable to human use. Nothing in this passage speaks directly to Job’s suffering, yet it shifts the frame: the world is not a moral machine centred on human beings. The ostrich passage says God “has made her forget wisdom” (39:17), a rare statement that some creatures lack human-style order, and yet they thrive.`,
      [[`Psalm 104:10-26`, `A creation psalm that includes wild goats, lions and Leviathan.`], [`Matthew 6:26`, `“Look at the birds of the air.”`]],
      [`God’s answer is a tour of untamed creatures. Why would that matter to a man in pain?`, `The world is full of life that has no use for humans. Is that comforting or disturbing?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the speech’s main effect?`, `To decentre human suffering in a vast creation.`, `To invite Job into a wider world of God’s care and delight.`]],
    [40, `Behemoth, and a challenge to Job’s judgement`,
      `God asks, “Shall a faultfinder contend with the Almighty?” and Job answers, “I am of small account; what shall I answer you? I lay my hand on my mouth” (40:2-4). God speaks again: “Will you even put me in the wrong? Will you condemn me that you may be in the right?” (40:8). Then he describes Behemoth, “which I made as I made you” (40:15), a creature that eats grass like an ox and has “bones like tubes of bronze.” Interpreters have proposed a hippopotamus, an elephant, or a mythic beast. The text says, “Behold, he is the first of the works of God; let him who made him bring near his sword” (40:19). The point is that some powers are beyond human control.`,
      [[`Isaiah 27:1`, `Leviathan as a symbol of chaos that God will slay.`], [`Psalm 74:13-14`, `God crushes the heads of Leviathan.`]],
      [`Job puts his hand on his mouth, and then God speaks again. What does that suggest about the aim of the speeches?`, `Does God’s question “Will you condemn me that you may be justified?” have a fair answer?`],
      [`choose`, `What is Behemoth?`, `A real animal such as a hippopotamus, described poetically.`, `A mythic creature, the first of God’s works.`]],
    [41, `Leviathan and the beginning of repentance`,
      `The second divine speech ends with Leviathan: “Can you draw out Leviathan with a fishhook?” (41:1). The poem lists scales like shields, breath like flames, and a heart “hard as a stone” (41:15-24). “On earth there is nothing like him, a creature without fear” (41:33). Leviathan appears in Ugaritic texts as a seven-headed sea serpent, and in Psalm 74:14 and Isaiah 27:1 as a monster of chaos. God’s claim in the book of Job is that this creature, too, belongs to his creation and plays under his rule (Psalm 104:26). Job’s answer comes in 42:1-6, “I had heard of you by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees you; therefore I despise myself, and repent in dust and ashes.”`,
      [[`Psalm 104:25-26`, `Leviathan as a creature that God made “to play.”`], [`Job 42:1-6`, `Job’s reply to God.`]],
      [`God shows Job the chaos monster as something he made to play with. What does that do to the idea of chaos?`, `Job’s answer follows. What does “now my eye sees you” mean?`],
      [`choose`, `What does Job’s repentance mean?`, `He repents of speaking without knowledge.`, `He submits to a God who has appeared and does not answer his question but answers him.`]]
  ],
  20: [
    [5, `A skeptic’s guide to worship and wealth`,
      `Ecclesiastes 5 starts with a warning for those who enter the house of God: “Guard your steps… to draw near to listen is better than to offer the sacrifice of fools” (5:1). “Let your words be few” (5:2), and if you make a vow, pay it (5:4-5). The passage then reflects on wealth: “He who loves money will not be satisfied with money” (5:10), and on labour: “Sweet is the sleep of a labouring man” (5:12). The Preacher observes that a person comes naked and leaves naked (5:15). His practical conclusion is that “it is good and fitting to eat and drink and find enjoyment in all the toil… for this is his lot” (5:18). The sceptic calls for sobriety in religion and gratitude for ordinary pleasure.`,
      [[`Matthew 6:7`, `Jesus: “do not heap up empty phrases.”`], [`1 Timothy 6:7-10`, `Paul on the love of money.`]],
      [`The Preacher says to listen more than to speak in worship. What would that look like in your own practice?`, `Is his approval of enjoyment a retreat from big questions or a form of wisdom?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read 5:18-20?`, `As a call to gratitude for what God gives.`, `As a stoic counsel to accept limits.`]]
  ]
};
